# Sports Remote bootstrap settings.
#
# Bare metal: /etc/sports-remote/env (chmod 600, root-owned; read by systemd).
# Docker:     the `environment:` block in docker-compose.yml, or `--env-file`.
#
# Only settings needed before the app can start live here. Everything else (services, TVs,
# users, Plex, ESPN key...) is configured in the app's setup screens and saved in DATA_DIR.
# Every line is optional; the defaults suit most homes.

# Port to listen on.
# PORT=8787

# Address to listen on. 0.0.0.0 = all interfaces; 127.0.0.1 = only this machine (use that when a
# reverse proxy or `tailscale serve` on the same machine is the only way in); or one LAN IP.
# BIND=0.0.0.0

# Where config.json, secrets.json and the ADB key live. The systemd unit and the Docker image set
# this already (/var/lib/sports-remote and /data); change it only if you know you need to.
# DATA_DIR=/var/lib/sports-remote

# Reverse proxies allowed to set X-Forwarded-For / X-Forwarded-Proto and identity headers such as
# Tailscale-User-Login or Remote-User. Comma-separated IPs or CIDRs, or the name "loopback".
# Ranges wider than /8 are refused, and setup refuses a range that contains this machine's own LAN address
# (list a LAN proxy by its single IP). Needed for proxy sign-in (e.g. loopback for `tailscale serve`).
# Leave empty if there is none. Never list a range that ordinary LAN clients can come from.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=loopback

# How people reach the app; used to print the one-time setup link on first start
# (journalctl -u sports-remote | grep setup_required, or docker logs).
# PUBLIC_URL=https://games.example.ts.net:10000

# Sign-ins last this many days since last use.
# SESSION_DAYS=30

# Host names the app may be reached by, comma-separated (blocks DNS-rebinding). Bare IPs,
# localhost and <name>.local are always allowed; add your proxy or Tailscale names here.
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=games.example.com,sports-remote.tailnet-name.ts.net

# How remote-control presses reach the TV, fastest first, over one adb shell that stays open.
# "evdev" writes key events straight to the TV's input device (about 10 ms; Android's `input` tool takes
# 1-3 s per press on a Fire TV). "cmd" is `cmd input` (Android 11+), "input" the classic tool. Each TV is
# checked and anything that doesn't work there is skipped. "oneshot" = a new `adb shell input keyevent` per press.
# KEY_METHODS=evdev,cmd,input

# There is no timezone setting here: game times and "Today / Tonight / Tomorrow" follow the timezone chosen
# in the app's setup ("Your area" step). Logs are ISO timestamps in UTC.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Legacy (migrated on first start)
#
# Installs from before 0.2 kept their settings here. When the new version first starts with
# these set, it imports them into DATA_DIR (see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md "Configuration"); after that
# they can be deleted from this file. New installs should leave them out and use the setup screens.
#
# FIRETV_HOST=192.168.1.50   # becomes the first TV (platform firetv, port 5555)
# PLEX_URL=http://127.0.0.1:32400
# PLEX_TOKEN=                # moved to secrets.json
# PLEX_PLAYER_ID=
# ESPN_WATCH_API_KEY=        # moved to secrets.json
#
# Not imported (the app logs a warning if they're still set): sign-in is chosen again through the
# one-time setup link, because 0.2 stores hashed PINs and passwords instead of plain values.
# SESSION_SECRET, APP_PASSCODE, ALLOWED_TAILNET_USERS, LAN_BIND (use BIND)
